bcast":false,"url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-18","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-19","committee":"Wiltshire Pension Fund Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-20","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed; Extraordinary Meeting","webcast":false,"url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Cabinet - Shareholder Group","time":"3.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Staffing Policy Committee","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Management Committee","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Wiltshire Pension Fund Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Wiltshire faces \u00a386m schools funding deficit waiting for government approval","lead":"Wiltshire Council's plan to balance its special needs education budget depends on approval from the Department for Education that has not yet come through. The council is holding a cumulative deficit of \u00a386.384m in its dedicated schools grant, the government funding that pays for schools, and can only access a grant covering 90 per cent of it if ministers approve its reform plan. The council expects to hear in September or October whether the plan has been approved. The pressures driving the deficit are intensifying: the number of children with education and care plans has grown 72 per cent in five years, from 4,158 to 7,149, and more of them have complex needs. Demand for specialist school places has outpaced the council's capital plans, which are now nearly fully committed with almost no budget buffer. If the Department for Education rejects the plan, the council would face the full \u00a386.384m exposure, which it warns could force \"difficult decisions about resources across the organisation\".","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Appendix 5 - LSRP Annex 4 - Capital Budget Summary (1)","paper_url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk/documents/s251748/251748.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Wiltshire faces \u00a386m schools funding deficit unless government approves SEND reform plan","lead":"Wiltshire Council is banking on government approval of its special educational needs reform plan to avoid a potential \u00a386.384m exposure to schools funding deficits. The council's cumulative deficit in its dedicated schools grant\u2014government funding for schools\u2014currently stands at \u00a386.384m after five years of rising demand. A national stability grant covering 90 per cent of this historic deficit is conditional on approval of the council's Local SEND Reform Plan, which the authority expects to hear about in September or October. If approval does not materialise, the council says it faces \"difficult decisions about resources across the organisation\". Meanwhile, Education, Health and Care Plan numbers have grown 72 per cent in five years, from 4,158 in March 2021 to 7,149 in March 2026, driven by increasing complexity of need. The council's capital spending plan for special schools faces separate pressures: major projects at Silverwood and Exeter House have both suffered substantial cost overruns, and the council is relying on further government grants to cover \u00a36.24m of a \u00a339.95m three-year capital plan.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Appendix 5 - LSRP Annex 4 - Capital Budget Summary (1)","paper_url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk/documents/s251748/251748.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Wiltshire faces \u00a380m school funding cliff as special needs demand soars and government approval uncertain","lead":"Wiltshire Council faces a sharp financial cliff in its special education budget, with deficits forecast to hit \u00a380.537m in 2027/28 as the statutory override period\u2014which currently allows the authority to defer schools-funding shortfalls\u2014ends on 31 March 2028. The council has seen Education, Health and Care Plan numbers rise 72 per cent in five years to 7,149, with children's needs becoming more complex: the proportion of pupils with the most complex needs in special schools doubled from 34 per cent to 72 per cent between 2021 and 2026. The council's recovery depends on government approval of a Local SEND Reform Plan, a decision it expects in September or October, and on receipt of a High Needs Stability Grant to cover 90 per cent of its accumulated \u00a386.384m deficit. The paper warns: failure to secure approval would expose the council \"to very significant pressure on the council's wider financial position and could require difficult decisions about resources across the organisation.\"","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Appendix 5 - LSRP Annex 4 - Capital Budget Summary (1)","paper_url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk/documents/s251748/251748.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Wiltshire SEND Reform Plan - May 2026 (1)","paper_url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk/documents/s251744/251744.pdf","headline":"The reform plan explicitly identifies financial sustainability as a key driver, aiming to 'stabilise the system financially' by shifting investment upstream","detail":"The plan states the reform will stabilise finances by shifting investment upstream and reducing reliance on high-cost specialist and independent placements, ensuring resources are used earlier where they have the greatest impact. This indicates the council views the current system as financially unsustainable.","category":"savings","matters":"A council identifying its own financial unsustainability and requiring systemic reform to stabilize finances indicates material budget pressures that could constrain its financial position","figures":"None provided in document","quote":"This reform will also stabilise the system financially and improve value for money by shifting investment upstream, reducing reliance on high-cost specialist and independent placements.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Wiltshire SEND Reform Plan - May 2026 (1)","paper_url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk/documents/s251744/251744.pdf","headline":"Special school populations have increased 63% since 2019 while mainstream has grown only 2%, with heavy reliance on expensive independent placements","detail":"The council notes that mainstream pupil growth is only 2% since 2019 compared to 63% growth in special schools. Wiltshire has comparatively low special school capacity and continued reliance on out-of-area independent placements. This is explicitly identified as creating financial and sufficiency pressure.","category":"overspend","matters":"Out-of-area independent placements cost significantly more than local provision; heavy reliance indicates substantial, ongoing budget pressure that affects reserve stability","figures":"Special school population increase +63% since 2019; mainstream pupil growth +2% since 2019","quote":"Special school populations have increased by 63% since 2019, reflecting system reliance on specialist provision. Wiltshire has comparatively low special school capacity and continued reliance on independent placements, creating financial and sufficiency pressure.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Wiltshire SEND Reform Plan - May 2026 (1)","paper_url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk/documents/s251744/251744.pdf","headline":"EHCP numbers have grown to 7,171 as of June 2026, increasing 6.3% year-on-year with growth concentrated in expensive need types","detail":"The council identifies sustained demand pressure driven by continued growth in EHCPs, particularly in neurodevelopmental and Social Emotional Mental Health (SEMH) needs. The five-year increase is concentrated in SEMH, speech/language/communication needs, and autism, which together account for 88.8% of all growth.","category":"demand","matters":"EHCP placements, especially those requiring specialist or independent provision, are among the most expensive services a council funds; sustained growth in expensive need types creates ongoing budget pressures affecting financial resilience","figures":"7,171 EHCPs as of June 2026; +6.3% year-on-year; 88.8% of five-year growth concentrated in SEMH, SLCN, and autism","quote":"Demand for EHCPs continues to rise, particularly in neurodevelopmental and Social Emotional Mental Health (SEMH) need. 7,171 as of June 2026, +6.3% year-on-year, with 5-year increase concentrated in SEMH, SLCN and Autism, together accounting for 88.8% of growth.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Financial Year 202526 - Quarter One Revenue Budget Monitoring - Master Report (1)","paper_url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk/documents/s251735/251735.pdf","headline":"Traded services income shortfall of \u00a30.196m from school academisation accelerating; HR delivery no longer cost-effective","detail":"Organisational Strategy & Digital forecasts \u00a30.196m traded income shortfall from 'continued academisation of schools and the resulting reduction in income'. The S151 flags that 'the cost of delivering HR to schools is currently not cost effective' and an audit is underway to assess this service line.","category":"income","matters":"Structural decline in council trading income from school autonomy trends, combined with fixed cost base, indicates permanent revenue erosion without mitigation strategy.","figures":"\u00a30.196m traded income shortfall; service under audit for cost-effectiveness","quote":"Risks remain in the service in relation to the traded income targets and the cost of delivering HR to schools is currently not cost effective, this is currently being audited and will need further consideration.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Financial Year 202526 - Quarter One Revenue Budget Monitoring - Master Report (1)","paper_url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk/documents/s251735/251735.pdf","headline":"SEN transport costs rising per pupil by 7.4% (\u00a3756 per child) despite lower volumes, due to fuel and market capacity constraints","detail":"SEN Transport forecast overspend of \u00a30.369m. Pupil numbers are 147 lower than budget (-5.6%), generating \u00a31.494m favourable variance, but average cost per pupil rises to \u00a310,921 vs budget \u00a310,165 (\u00a3756 increase, 7.4%), creating \u00a31.865m adverse variance. Drivers include route cost increases, fuel inflation, market capacity constraints, and increased pupil needs complexity.","category":"demand","matters":"Rising per-unit costs despite lower demand indicates capacity constraints and inflationary pressures outside council control; savings targets in this area appear unachievable.","figures":"\u00a30.369m overspend; average cost per pupil \u00a310,921 (+\u00a3756); 147 fewer pupils","quote":"The increase in unit costs more than offsets the savings from lower pupil numbers, leading to a net forecast overspend of \u00a30.369m. The underlying cost pressures are driven by increased route costs, changes in pupil needs and provision requirements, fuel cost inflationary pressures within transport contracts, and market capacity challenges","material":false}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"Wiltshire UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-25","since_iso":"2026-08-26","distress":false,"headline":"The reform plan explicitly identifies financial sustainability as a key driver, aiming to 'stabilise the system financially' by shifting investment upstream","lead":"A council identifying its own financial unsustainability and requiring systemic reform to stabilize finances indicates material budget pressures that could constrain its financial position","also":["48 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit and Governance Committee on 2026-10-06","n_findings":49,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Wiltshire SEND Reform Plan - May 2026 (1)","top_paper_url":"https://cms.wiltshire.gov.uk/documents/s251744/251744.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":143,"news_drafts_total":3}}